From 503b7d8517aafc2980bbd70e34f49580978898d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 00:24:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CUDA/HIP: narrow mmvq blocks whose trailing warps get no
 k-blocks

At batch 1, mmvq hands each thread a slice of a k-block, so a thread block is
only fully fed when

    blocks_per_row_x >= nwarps * blocks_per_iter_1warp

On the RDNA4 table calc_nwarps() returns 8 for q4_K and q6_K at ncols_dst=1.
For q4_K, vdr=2 and qi=32 give blocks_per_iter_1warp=2, so a full 8-warp block
wants 16 k-blocks - but every K=2048 tensor in LFM2.5-2.6B has only 8. Warps
4-7 start past the end of the row, fall straight through to the reduction
without ever entering the k-loop, and still cost a full wave of occupancy each.

q6_K has vdr=1 and therefore wants exactly 8 k-blocks, which is what K=2048
supplies. That asymmetry is visible end to end on the R9700: the 128000-row
q6_K output head is the only matvec in this model whose block is already fully
fed, and it is also the only one anywhere near peak bandwidth.

  stock, per decode token (rocprofv3 kernel trace, 129 token-steps)

    tensor                    rows      K   warps   us each   GB/s
    ffn up+gate (fused)      10752   2048       8      65.8    377
    shortconv in_proj         6144   2048       8      23.3    304
    out_proj / q / o          2048   2048       8       9.4    ~390
    ffn_down                  2048  10752       8      27.0    ~460
    output head (q6_K)      128000   2048       8     380.5    565

Upstream already computes this condition - should_use_small_k() tests exactly
blocks_per_row_x < nwarps*blocks_per_iter_1warp - but the remedy behind it
widens rows_per_cuda_block, which hands warp 0 more rows and still never gives
the trailing warps any k-blocks. calc_rows_per_block() returns 1 on every RDNA
table anyway, and the whole path is vetoed for RDNA, so on these parts the flag
is dead.

Since rows_per_cuda_block is fixed at 1 on RDNA, the small_k template slot is
free to mean "narrow the block" rather than "widen the rows": halve nwarps so
every launched lane still receives a k-block, and twice as many blocks stay
resident per CU. This reuses the existing template parameter, so it adds no new
kernel instantiations, and it leaves the GENERIC/GCN/TURING meaning untouched.

Two conditions gate it, both derived from geometry rather than from a device id:

  1. the narrowed block must still cover every k-block
     (nwarps/2)*blocks_per_iter_1warp <= blocks_per_row_x
  2. the narrowed launch must still fill the device with slack for latency
     nrows_x*(nwarps/2) >= nsm*128 waves

Condition 2 is not optional. Narrowing halves the threads per block without
changing the block count, so it trades total parallelism for occupancy, and
that is the wrong trade when there are too few blocks to fill the machine. The
512-row k/v projections (nrows_x=512, K=2048, q4_K) supply exactly nsm*64 waves
after narrowing - one bare pass over the device, no slack - and narrowing them
measured a 2.8x regression on that shape (4.1 -> 11.6 us) while every larger
shape gained. Requiring twice a full pass keeps them on the stock geometry.

Resulting geometry on this model - only the shapes that are both starved and
large enough change:

    tensor                    rows      K   warps      us each      GB/s
    ffn up+gate (fused)      10752   2048   8 -> 4   65.8 -> 42.6  377 -> 582
    shortconv in_proj         6144   2048   8 -> 4   23.3 -> 14.7  304 -> 483
    out_proj / q / o          2048   2048   8 -> 4    9.4 ->  6.3
    ffn_down                  2048  10752   8         27.0         K long enough
    k/v proj                   512   2048   8          3.2         too few blocks
    output head (q6_K)      128000   2048   8        360.4         already fed

    mul_mat_vec_q total   4101.7 -> 3062.7 us/token  (-25.3%)
    all kernels           4802.6 -> 3730.0 us/token  (-22.3%)

Correctness: bit-identical, not merely within tolerance. blocks_per_iter is
still derived from the compile-time nwarps of the launched block, and the
threads that survive keep exactly the k-block assignment they had in stock -
warps 4-7 contributed +0.0f to the shared-memory reduction and contributing
nothing instead leaves the same summation with the same operand order. Measured
on the fixed corpus: PPL 18.3020 +/- 1.31964 stock and 18.3020 +/- 1.31964
patched, identical in every one of the 13 chunk values (gate allows 0.5%).

Speed on the R9700, llama-bench, 7 interleaved whole-process launches per arm
with rotated order, median of per-round ratios, same-binary env toggle:

    decode  180.325 -> 211.439 tok/s   ratio 1.17177  (+17.177%)
    prefill                            ratio 1.00018
    per-round decode ratios: 1.16914 1.17054 1.17082 1.17177 1.17178 1.17255 1.17325

    same-binary no-op control (both arms stock): 1.00094, range 0.99931-1.00229

Prefill is untouched because the change is confined to ncols_dst == 1.

Portability. The gate is geometric, so it fires only where the arithmetic says
warps are starved; nothing keys off gfx1201. On the RDNA2 table - which covers
RDNA2 and RDNA3.5 - q4_K uses nwarps=1 and q6_K nwarps=2 with vdr=1, so neither
type is starved and this is a no-op for this model on those parts. GGML_CUDA_-
MMVQ_NO_NARROW=1 restores the stock geometry at runtime for A/B and bisection.

Prior art: the idle-warp root cause and a launch-trim remedy were established
on the sibling laguna-xs-2.1-gguf-r9700-v1 series (0001-mmvq-trim-idle-warps,
0002-mmvq-remove-launch-trim-floor), which trims blockDim.y to the exact warp
count. That series is not applied to this track, whose frontier is pinned
b10237. On LFM2.5's tensor shapes exact trim and halving select the same warp
counts (q4_K K=2048 -> 4 either way), so the difference here is the occupancy
floor, which those patches do not have and which is worth 2.9 points of the
17.2 measured above.
---
 ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmvq.cu | 57 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 51 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmvq.cu b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmvq.cu
index 0589e65bd..a295996d4 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmvq.cu
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmvq.cu
@@ -351,7 +351,7 @@ static constexpr __device__ int get_mmvq_mmid_max_batch_for_device() {
 #endif
 }
 
-static constexpr __host__ __device__ int calc_nwarps(ggml_type type, int ncols_dst, mmvq_parameter_table_id table_id) {
+static constexpr __host__ __device__ int calc_nwarps_base(ggml_type type, int ncols_dst, mmvq_parameter_table_id table_id) {
     if (table_id == MMVQ_PARAMETERS_GENERIC) {
         switch (ncols_dst) {
             case 1:
@@ -457,6 +457,26 @@ static constexpr __host__ __device__ int calc_nwarps(ggml_type type, int ncols_d
     return 1;
 }
 
+// A batch-1 matvec block hands each thread a slice of a k-block, so a full-width block can
+// only be kept busy when blocks_per_row_x >= nwarps*blocks_per_iter_1warp. When K is too
+// short for that, the surplus warps exit the k-loop immediately and do nothing except
+// consume per-CU occupancy - which is what actually limits a bandwidth-bound matvec.
+// On the RDNA tables calc_rows_per_block() is always 1, so the small_k template slot is
+// free to mean "narrow the block" instead of "widen the rows": halve the warp count so
+// every lane still receives a k-block and twice as many blocks stay resident per CU.
+static constexpr __host__ __device__ bool is_rdna_table(mmvq_parameter_table_id table_id) {
+    return table_id == MMVQ_PARAMETERS_RDNA4 || table_id == MMVQ_PARAMETERS_RDNA3_0 ||
+           table_id == MMVQ_PARAMETERS_RDNA2;
+}
+
+static constexpr __host__ __device__ int calc_nwarps(ggml_type type, int ncols_dst,
+                                                     mmvq_parameter_table_id table_id,
+                                                     bool narrow_k = false) {
+    return (narrow_k && is_rdna_table(table_id) && calc_nwarps_base(type, ncols_dst, table_id) > 1)
+        ? calc_nwarps_base(type, ncols_dst, table_id) / 2
+        : calc_nwarps_base(type, ncols_dst, table_id);
+}
+
 static constexpr __host__ __device__ int calc_rows_per_block(int ncols_dst, int table_id, bool small_k = false, int nwarps = 1) {
     if (table_id == MMVQ_PARAMETERS_GENERIC || table_id == MMVQ_PARAMETERS_GCN || table_id == MMVQ_PARAMETERS_TURING) {
         switch (ncols_dst) {
@@ -478,7 +498,7 @@ static constexpr __host__ __device__ int calc_rows_per_block(int ncols_dst, int
 }
 
 template <ggml_type type, int ncols_dst, bool has_fusion, bool small_k = false>
-__launch_bounds__(calc_nwarps(type, ncols_dst, get_device_table_id())*ggml_cuda_get_physical_warp_size(), 1)
+__launch_bounds__(calc_nwarps(type, ncols_dst, get_device_table_id(), small_k)*ggml_cuda_get_physical_warp_size(), 1)
 static __global__ void mul_mat_vec_q(
         const void * vx_ptr, const void * vy_ptr, const int32_t * ids_ptr, const ggml_cuda_mm_fusion_args_device fusion, float * dst_ptr,
         const uint32_t ncols_x, const uint3 nchannels_y, const uint32_t stride_row_x, const uint32_t stride_col_y,
@@ -495,7 +515,7 @@ static __global__ void mul_mat_vec_q(
     constexpr int qi  = ggml_cuda_type_traits<type>::qi;
     constexpr int vdr = get_vdr_mmvq(type);
     constexpr mmvq_parameter_table_id table_id = get_device_table_id();
-    constexpr int nwarps = calc_nwarps(type, ncols_dst, table_id);
+    constexpr int nwarps = calc_nwarps(type, ncols_dst, table_id, small_k);
     constexpr int rows_per_cuda_block = calc_rows_per_block(ncols_dst, table_id, small_k, nwarps);
     constexpr int warp_size = ggml_cuda_get_physical_warp_size();
 
@@ -774,7 +794,7 @@ template<ggml_type type>
 static std::pair<dim3, dim3> calc_launch_params(
         const int ncols_dst, const int nrows_x, const int nchannels_dst, const int nsamples_or_ntokens,
         const int warp_size, const mmvq_parameter_table_id table_id, const bool small_k = false) {
-    const int nwarps = calc_nwarps(type, ncols_dst, table_id);
+    const int nwarps = calc_nwarps(type, ncols_dst, table_id, small_k);
     const int rpb = calc_rows_per_block(ncols_dst, table_id, small_k, nwarps);
     const int64_t nblocks = (nrows_x + rpb - 1) / rpb;
     const dim3 block_nums(nblocks, nchannels_dst, nsamples_or_ntokens);
@@ -894,9 +914,34 @@ static void mul_mat_vec_q_switch_ncols_dst(
                 use = false;
             }
         } else if ((ncols_dst == 1 && std::find(iq_slow_other.begin(), iq_slow_other.end(), type) != iq_slow_other.end()) ||
-                (is_nvidia_pascal_older && std::find(slow_pascal.begin(), slow_pascal.end(), type) != slow_pascal.end()) ||
-                GGML_CUDA_CC_IS_RDNA(cc)) {
+                (is_nvidia_pascal_older && std::find(slow_pascal.begin(), slow_pascal.end(), type) != slow_pascal.end())) {
             use = false;
+        } else if (GGML_CUDA_CC_IS_RDNA(cc)) {
+            // On RDNA small_k narrows the block (halves nwarps) instead of widening rows.
+            // Only take it when the narrowed block still covers every k-block, so that no
+            // lane goes idle in the narrowed launch either; otherwise keep stock geometry.
+            static const bool narrow_disabled = [] {
+                const char * e = getenv("GGML_CUDA_MMVQ_NO_NARROW");
+                return e != nullptr && atoi(e) != 0;
+            }();
+
+            // Narrowing halves the threads per block without changing the block count, so it
+            // trades total parallelism for per-CU occupancy. That is only the right trade when
+            // enough waves remain to fill the device: at small nrows_x the launch is limited by
+            // total waves in flight rather than by occupancy, and halving them costs more than
+            // the idle lanes did. Measured on gfx1201, nrows_x=512 K=2048 q4_K: narrowing there
+            // is a 2.8x regression on that shape while every larger shape gains.
+            //
+            // A scheduling unit holds 2048 threads = 64 waves of 32 on both vendors (an AMD WGP
+            // as reported by multiProcessorCount, an NVIDIA SM), so nsm*64 waves is one full
+            // pass over the device. Require twice that, so there is still slack to cover memory
+            // latency after the narrowing.
+            const int64_t narrowed_waves = int64_t(nrows_x) * (nwarps / 2);
+            const int64_t waves_to_fill  = int64_t(ggml_cuda_info().devices[device].nsm) * 128;
+
+            use = use && !narrow_disabled && nwarps >= 2 &&
+                  (nwarps / 2) * blocks_per_iter_1warp <= blocks_per_row_x &&
+                  narrowed_waves >= waves_to_fill;
         }
 
         return use;
-- 
2.53.0

